# HCW: Purity Under Concurrency

```elixir
project_path = System.get_env("PLUMBLINE_PROJECT_PATH") || Path.join(__DIR__, "..")

Mix.install([{:plumbline, path: project_path}])
```

## What this document is

Every other gate in this repository runs the implementation one call at
a time, in one process. This one runs it many at a time, in many
processes, and asserts that nothing changes.

The property under test is **purity**: the same inputs must produce the
same outputs, always, regardless of what else is running. Nothing in
the interface contract promises this in so many words, and no check
elsewhere can see it. An implementation that memoises into the process
dictionary, an ETS table, or `:persistent_term` satisfies every
invariant in this repository — the physics is right, the oracle agrees,
the invariants hold — and then returns a stale answer the first time
two callers overlap.

That failure does not appear in development, appears intermittently
under load, and is not reproducible from the inputs alone. It is
exactly the class of defect that reaches production intact.

Every assertion here demands **exact equality**. Purity is not a
quantity with a tolerance: results either match or the implementation
has state in it.

Gated separately, with its own history:

```sh
bin/run_checkpoint livebooks/hcw_concurrency.livemd
elixir bin/checkpoint_history.exs hcw_concurrency
```

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.join(project_path, "livebooks/checkpoint.exs"))

Checkpoint.init([
  "concurrent_matches_sequential",
  "concurrent_result_is_deterministic",
  "shared_input_yields_identical_results",
  "survivors_unaffected_by_sibling_crash"
])
```

## Workload

Distinct states, enough of them and enough work in each that the
schedulers genuinely interleave rather than finishing one before the
next begins.

```elixir
alias Plumbline.Dynamics.HCW

seed = 20_260_905
:rand.seed(:exsss, {seed, seed, seed})

tasks_count = 32
steps = 2_000

mu = 398_600.4418
n = :math.sqrt(mu / :math.pow(6378.137 + 400.0, 3))
period = 2 * :math.pi() / n
dt = period / steps

uniform = fn lo, hi -> lo + :rand.uniform() * (hi - lo) end

states =
  for _ <- 1..tasks_count do
    v = n * 10.0

    %HCW.State{
      x: uniform.(-10.0, 10.0),
      y: uniform.(-10.0, 10.0),
      z: uniform.(-10.0, 10.0),
      vx: uniform.(-v, v),
      vy: uniform.(-v, v),
      vz: uniform.(-v, v)
    }
  end

work = fn state -> HCW.propagate(state, n, dt, period) end

in_parallel = fn inputs ->
  inputs
  |> Task.async_stream(work, ordered: true, timeout: 120_000, max_concurrency: System.schedulers_online())
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, result} -> result end)
end

%{schedulers: System.schedulers_online(), tasks: tasks_count, steps_each: steps}
```

## Concurrent against sequential

```elixir
sequential = Enum.map(states, work)
parallel = in_parallel.(states)

matches_sequential? = sequential == parallel

Checkpoint.check("concurrent_matches_sequential", matches_sequential?,
  expected: true,
  actual: matches_sequential?,
  unit: "boolean",
  message: "#{tasks_count} trajectories computed concurrently must equal the same trajectories computed one at a time"
)
```

## The same work twice

Running concurrently once and getting the right answer can be luck: a
particular interleaving that happened not to collide. Two runs of the
same workload must agree with each other as well, because a shared
mutable cell produces results that depend on scheduling rather than on
inputs.

```elixir
deterministic? = in_parallel.(states) == parallel

Checkpoint.check("concurrent_result_is_deterministic", deterministic?,
  expected: true,
  actual: deterministic?,
  unit: "boolean",
  message: "the identical concurrent workload run twice must produce identical results; disagreement means the answer depends on scheduling"
)
```

## One input, many processes at once

The sharpest form: every process asks the same question simultaneously.
A cache keyed too loosely, or written without regard for who else is
reading, shows up here as processes disagreeing about a single answer.

```elixir
one_state = List.first(states)
answers = in_parallel.(List.duplicate(one_state, tasks_count))
distinct = answers |> Enum.uniq() |> length()

Checkpoint.check("shared_input_yields_identical_results", distinct == 1,
  expected: 1,
  actual: distinct,
  unit: "distinct results",
  message: "#{tasks_count} processes propagating the same state must produce exactly one distinct answer between them"
)
```

## Survivors of a sibling crash

Tasks are started under a supervisor and some are killed part-way. The
ones that live must finish with the answers they would have produced
alone. A pure computation has nothing for a dying neighbour to corrupt;
shared state does.

```elixir
{:ok, supervisor} = Task.Supervisor.start_link()

running =
  for state <- states do
    Task.Supervisor.async_nolink(supervisor, fn -> work.(state) end)
  end

# Kill every third task while the rest are still going.
running
|> Enum.with_index()
|> Enum.each(fn {task, index} -> if rem(index, 3) == 0, do: Process.exit(task.pid, :kill) end)

outcomes = Task.yield_many(running, 120_000)

survivors_correct? =
  outcomes
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.all?(fn
    {{_task, {:ok, result}}, index} -> result == Enum.at(sequential, index)
    {{_task, _dead_or_pending}, _index} -> true
  end)

survivor_count = Enum.count(outcomes, fn {_, outcome} -> match?({:ok, _}, outcome) end)

Supervisor.stop(supervisor)

IO.puts("  #{survivor_count} of #{tasks_count} tasks survived")

Checkpoint.check("survivors_unaffected_by_sibling_crash", survivors_correct? and survivor_count > 0,
  expected: true,
  actual: survivors_correct?,
  unit: "boolean",
  message: "tasks that outlive a killed sibling must return exactly what they would have returned alone (#{survivor_count} survivors checked)"
)
```

```elixir
Checkpoint.complete()
```
